# Performance-First Coding

Whenever creating or changing code, design for performance so the app stays fast under real load. Prefer correct + efficient defaults over premature micro-optimization, but never ship obvious N+1, unbounded queries, or heavy work on the hot path.

## Backend (Django / DRF)

- Avoid N+1: use `select_related` / `prefetch_related` for list/detail serializers and admin inlines.
- Never load unbounded querysets into memory; paginate list APIs and admin changelists.
- Put filters/lookups on indexed fields; add DB indexes for frequent filter/order pairs.
- Keep business logic out of tight request loops; batch writes with `bulk_create` / `bulk_update` when inserting many rows.
- Do not call external payment/HTTP APIs inside DB transactions longer than necessary; commit first when safe.
- Offload slow work (email, reports, heavy sync) to Celery — not request/response.
- Cache only stable, read-heavy data (settings, gateway catalog); invalidate on write.
- Log without dumping large payloads or secrets.

```python
# ❌ BAD — N+1
for order in Order.objects.all():
    print(order.user.email)

# ✅ GOOD
for order in Order.objects.select_related("user").all()[:100]:
    print(order.user.email)
```

## Frontend (React / Vite)

- Fetch only what the page needs; avoid refetching on every unrelated render.
- List UIs: paginate/virtualize long lists; do not render huge arrays at once.
- Debounce search inputs; cancel or ignore stale responses.
- Prefer CSS/Tailwind for animation; avoid layout thrash in scroll handlers.
- Do not put large objects in React state if a ref or derived value is enough.
- Images: sensible sizes, lazy-load below the fold when lists grow.

```jsx
# ❌ BAD — search fires every keystroke without debounce
useEffect(() => { api.search(q) }, [q])

# ✅ GOOD — debounce before requesting
useEffect(() => {
  const t = setTimeout(() => api.search(q), 300)
  return () => clearTimeout(t)
}, [q])
```

## General

- Measure the hot path before clever tricks; fix algorithmic waste first (O(n²), duplicate work).
- Fail closed on huge inputs (max page size, upload size, payload limits).
- New endpoints/features must consider: query count, payload size, and concurrency — not only happy-path correctness.
